A||||4||DavidHenshaw||Steering wheel shimmy||||1||
Z||000000||DavidHenshaw||02-20-2001||11:13 AM||||When driving at higher speeds if the highway isn't nice and smooth I get a shimmy in the steering wheel. What is the most likely cause. Car has 211,000 miles and I don't think anything up front has ever been replaced.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Bob Englert||02-20-2001||03:34 PM||||Sounds to me like you answered your own question!<P>I have had several Fieros with about 100K and every one of them had worn out front suspension components.  Jack the front wheels off the ground and grab and shake the tires and wheels.  See if you feel any meovement in any direction.  I would suspect the control arm bushings and possibly the ball joints are worn out.  You also might have play in the tie rods or rack components.<P>In any case, with 211K a front end rebuild might not be a bad idea.  Most of my Fieros handled MUCH better (felt much more solid) after renewing all the front end components.<P>No one else can really answer your question.  You need to get underneath and see what is loose and where the play is.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||02-20-2001||07:14 PM||||start with the easiest and cheapest...are the tires round and balanced?||||reg||1
Z||000003||DavidHenshaw||02-21-2001||10:59 AM||||Thanks Roger and Bob.<P>The tires are fine so it must be worn front end parts.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||02-21-2001||08:46 PM||||I'd start with the tires like was said. I've had tires that looked perfect, but found out they were out of round, out of balance, and in one case, a tire had a slipped belt that caused a vibration only at a certain speed range.<P>I've had to replace front suspension and steering parts in all three of the Fieros that I've owned. But, don't forget about the shocks and struts or the rear ball joints too. One Fiero that I used to have was driving me crazy with a front end vibration. Turned out to be mostly rear struts.||||reg||1
